DATASETS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sudoku puzzle sets are compiled from publicly available community collections
(forum.enjoysudoku.com "hardest" threads; the public "top1465" set).

LICENSING STATUS OF THE DATASETS: UNRESOLVED -- and deliberately treated the
same strict way as the third-party sources above.

  The upstream publishers attached NO licence, authorisation, or redistribution
  condition to these puzzle collections. Under the same "no licence specified
  means do not redistribute" standard that this NOTICE applies to jczsolve, the
  datasets must therefore NOT be assumed redistributable either.

  We do not rely on the argument that "puzzle strings are factual data":
  the selection and compilation (e.g. the SER>=11 sampling of sample5000, the
  curation of top1465) may attract database or compilation rights, and the
  answer differs by jurisdiction. In a peer-review context, an unverified
  provenance is itself a risk regardless of the legal conclusion.

  Size of the exposure: ~6.0 MB, 75,831 instances, ~50,231 distinct puzzle
  strings. Only top1465_clean.txt (2.0% by size) comes from a genuinely
  independent source; everything else derives from the 48,766-instance forum
  corpus.

  CONSEQUENCE FOR DISTRIBUTION:
    * NEITHER the public repository NOR the release archive ships data/*.txt.
    * Instead, out/fetch_data.py downloads the upstream tdoku data.zip
      (verified by git blob SHA1 against the GitHub API), rebuilds all five
      datasets locally by exact rules, and checks each one byte-for-byte
      against its published MD5. Verified: 5/5 match.
    * Upstream provenance and the exact reconstruction rules are documented
      in DATA_NOTICE.md; if the network is unavailable, contact the author.

  Full provenance, digests, and a three-tier mitigation menu:
  see DATA_NOTICE.md.

CAVEAT ON INTERPRETATION: With the sole exception of top1465_clean.txt, all
included datasets derive from the same source population. Any generalisation
claim built on f20k or indep600 as an "independent test" is therefore invalid
-- it is same-distribution held-out data, not cross-population generalisation.
See THIRD_PARTY.md §3.

